    "One page of screenplay roughly equals one minute of screen time.",
    "Spec scripts carry detailed shot directions, since the writer is planning the camera work.",
    "Lean action lines and generous white space are pace made visible.",
    "Non-standard format reads as amateur before the story gets a chance."
  ],
  "errorLine": 2,
  "correction": "Spec scripts carry no shot directions — that vocabulary belongs to the shooting script.",
  "why": "Readers triage hundreds of scripts; a script is a machine for convincing busy people, and format is the first filter."
};

const swBirdsOrder = {
  "prompt": "Pixar's wordless three-act short — put the beats in order:",
  "items": [
    "Small birds on a wire — a status order established",
    "A big, gangly bird lands mid-wire — the small ones crowd away",
    "The want escalates: get him off",
    "They peck his feet until he drops — turn one",
    "The wire, relieved of his weight, recoils — turn two",
    "Naked, featherless birds in the grass as one feather drifts down"
  ],
  "why": "One premise, one turn, no B-stories — the entire craft in miniature, staged shot by shot."
};

const swBoardCloze = {
  "text": "The digital workflow adds %%0%% — the boards cut together with scratch sound and timed, really a first edit you can watch before shooting a frame. The board forces three early decisions: %%1%% (who owns the frame and when), %%2%% (staging the 180-degree line before the shoot, not during), and coverage (whether the scene's turns are photographable at all).",
  "answers": [
    [
      "animatic",
      "animatics"
    ],
    [
      [
        "shot size",
        "shot-size"
      ]
    ],
    [
      "axis"
    ]
  ],
  "hint": "A storyboard is the screenplay's second draft in pictures."
};

const swShortPredict = {
  "q": "A writer treats a short film as a compressed feature, opening with a world-building first act. What happens?",
  "choices": [
    "The short breathes — features and shorts reward the same patience",
    "The worst of both: a premise too big for its runtime, a runtime too long for its premise",
    "Nothing — festivals reward ambition"
  ],
  "answer": 1,
  "why": "A short must open with its conflict already running and reach a turn inside minutes — one premise, one turn, no B-stories."
};

const swTenPageEstimate = {
  "prompt": "Development readers triage hard. If premise, voice and control have not appeared, by roughly what page does the script rarely get another chance?",
  "unit": "pages in",
  "answer": 10,
  "factor": 1.3,
  "why": "The ten-page reality: without premise, voice and control by then, the script rarely gets page eleven.",
  "context": "The Black List (Franklin Leonard's annual survey, running since 2005) exists because verdicts are opinions with a track record — not measurements."
};

const swCriteriaMatch = {
  "prompt": "Five defensible criteria — pair each with the question it asks:",
  "pairs": [
    {
      "left": "the logline test",
      "right": "can the premise be stated in two sentences with protagonist, goal and obstacle?"
    },
    {
      "left": "the scene-turn test",
      "right": "does each scene change a value — info, power, safety, intimacy?"
    },
    {
      "left": "the want/need audit",
      "right": "is the external goal visibly different from the internal lesson?"
    },
    {
      "left": "the name-covering dialogue test",
      "right": "cover the names — can you still tell who is speaking?"
    },
    {
      "left": "the theme audit",
      "right": "what does the ending actually argue, and did the climax earn it?"
    }
  ],
  "why": "The beats are diagnostic instruments — they locate where a draft's energy sags, but they cannot create the thing they measure."
};

const swDinnerCompare = {
  "prompt": "Two kinds of scene turn — drag the divider:",
  "before": {
    "label": "plot-shaped",
    "text": "What the template expects: events collide, a value flips, the scene ends changed. By that ruler, two men talking over dinner for an entire film should be killed on the page."
  },
  "after": {
    "label": "positional",
    "text": "What My Dinner with André does instead: Wally resists André's spiritual odyssey beat by beat, and the value at stake — how to live — shifts from defence to reflection to the taxi ride home, where the coffee cup is rediscovered with new eyes."
  },
  "why": "The criteria are real and the criteria have limits — a template would have passed on this film, and missed it."
};

const swNotesBranch = {
  "root": "You are in the development meeting. The reader across the table says: 'the second act drags.'",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "text": "What do you do with the note?",
      "choices": [
        {
          "label": "Go tighten the second act",
          "next": "symptom",
          "feedback": "You treated the report, not the patient."
        },
        {
          "label": "Diagnose before editing — where does the want break down?",
          "next": "diagnose",
          "feedback": "The professional move: separate the note from the disease."
        },
        {
          "label": "Dismiss it — verdicts are just opinions",
          "next": "dismiss",
          "feedback": "Half right, which is the dangerous half."
        }
      ]
    },
    "symptom": {
      "text": "The fix usually lives somewhere else entirely — a mis-chosen midpoint, or a protagonist without a want in Act One — and the second act still sags in the next draft."
    },
    "diagnose": {
      "text": "Run the classic failure modes: the passive protagonist (things happen to them until page ninety), the theme stated instead of dramatised, the antagonist who is only as smart as the plot requires."
    },
    "dismiss": {
      "text": "Verdicts are opinions with a track record, true — but good notes describe real experience ('I stopped worrying about her here'), and experience is data."
    }
  },
  "why": "Good note-takers describe their experience rather than prescribe rewrites; good note-takers-on diagnose before editing."
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/film/advanced/screenwriting-craft">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is an advanced lesson in Film, and it stands slightly apart from the camera lessons
    that came before: here the film does not exist yet, and the craft is designing the machine
    that will become one. The foundational lesson Grammar of cinema showed you how a finished
    film's grammar works on an audience; the intermediate lessons — Shot sizes and camera
    grammar, Editing the cut, Sound design and score, Cinematography and lighting — trained
    the channels that realise a script on screen. This lesson backs up the pipeline to the
    page: what a screenplay must do, in the industry's formats, before any of those channels
    exist.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>explain why screenwriting is a craft of persuasion aimed at busy professional readers, and why format is the first filter;</li>
    <li>use the structural canon — three acts, plot points, the eight-sequence method — without confusing a template with the drama it measures;</li>
    <li>design scenes that turn: want versus obstacle, a value that shifts, dialogue as action on a listener;</li>
    <li>plan a short film and its storyboard, including the animatic as a first editable cut;</li>
    <li>evaluate any script — yours or a professional's — with five defensible criteria, and give notes the way the profession does.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Screenwriting is <strong>dramaturgy in the industry's formats</strong> — scene craft, dialogue
    as action, and structure as engineered tension. The formats (beats, sequences) are scaffolds
    for the literature lesson's age-old engine: desire, obstacle, change.
  </p>
  <p>
    What makes screenwriting its own craft rather than prose with dialogue is the reader's
    reality: a script is a machine for convincing people who have a hundred scripts to read.
    Everything in this lesson — the format discipline, the beat architecture, the storyboard —
    exists because attention is the scarcest resource in the pipeline, and a screenplay that
    wastes a page loses its audience before page ten. The advanced writer holds both truths at
    once: the templates are real (readers genuinely expect them), and the templates are not the
    drama (no beat sheet ever made a scene true).
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="structure, scene turns, dialogue-as-action, character choice" widget="equationStep" config={scriptCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> step through the four panels in order and watch the lens
    tighten. Panel one is the whole film's architecture; panel two shrinks the film to one
    scene's want-versus-obstacle; panel three narrows to the line, where speech is action
    performed on a listener; panel four reaches the person underneath — character as choice
    under cost. The panels are nested, not parallel: a structural sag usually turns out to be
    a scene that does not turn, and a dead scene usually turns out to be a character who
    wants nothing. That nesting is the diagnostic habit the evaluation section formalises.
  </p>

  <h2>Short film and screenplay craft — with the digital storyboard</h2>
  <p>
    Start with the format, because format is the profession's first filter. Courier 12-point,
    one page ≈ one minute of screen time, present tense, and only what a camera can see or a
    speaker can say; spec scripts (written on speculation, unsold) carry no shot directions —
    that vocabulary belongs to the shooting script. Lean action lines and generous white space
    are pace made visible. These are not aesthetic preferences: readers triage hundreds of
    scripts, and non-standard format reads as amateur before the story gets a chance.
  </p>
  <Widget no="2" caption="find the faulty line — the format briefing" widget="spot-error" config={swFormatSpot} />
  <p>
    The development pipeline has its own economics worth knowing. A studio usually
    <strong>options</strong> a script — exclusive rights to attempt production for a fixed
    term, for a fee commonly cited at a small percentage of the eventual purchase price —
    rather than buying it outright; the script may be optioned repeatedly, rewritten by
    successive writers, and never filmed, which is why professionals describe sold scripts
    and made films as different prizes. The writer's craft within that pipeline is partly
    administrative: a tight logline travels through pitch meetings intact, a readable draft
    survives coverage, and knowing what each draft is <em>for</em> (the exploratory draft,
    the pitch draft, the production draft) keeps a career sane. Screenwriting is the only
    writing form whose first reader is a system — learn the system without letting it write
    the script for you.
  </p>
  <p>
    The structural canon, in one pass: Syd Field's <em>Screenplay</em> (1979) fixed the
    three-act paradigm with plot points near the quarter and three-quarter marks; the
    eight-sequence method (Frank Daniel's teaching lineage) treats a film as eight
    mini-movies, each with its own turn; Blake Snyder's <em>Save the Cat</em> (2005) gave
    commercials-and-cartoons precision with fifteen named beats. Under all of them sits the
    real engine: <strong>a character wants something, something blocks it, and the outcome
    changes a value the audience can feel</strong> (safer into danger, ignorance into
    knowledge). Dialogue obeys the same law — speech is action performed on the listener —
    and the exposition test is brutal: any line that exists to inform the audience rather than
    to move the character ("as you know, my sister…") is a line the scene doesn't need.
    Character is choice under cost: the midpoint and the "all is lost" beat exist to force
    decisions that redefine the protagonist, and the classic bittersweet ending grants the
    character's <em>need</em> while withholding the <em>want</em>.
  </p>
  <p>
    The short film is its own discipline, not a small feature: one premise, one turn, no
    B-stories — festival and award rules generally cap shorts at forty minutes, and the
    strongest run five to fifteen. Pixar's <em>For the Birds</em> (2000, an Oscar two years
    later) is a complete three-act structure in about three minutes, wordless: small birds on
    a wire (setup — a status order established); a big, gangly bird lands mid-wire and the
    small ones crowd away (inciting incident and escalating want: get him off); they peck his
    feet until he drops (turn one); the wire — relieved of his weight — recoils (turn two:
    the physics reversal nobody priced in); naked, featherless small birds perch in the grass
    as one feather drifts down (resolution and punchline). Map those beats onto the paradigm
    and you have the entire craft in miniature — which is exactly what the syllabus exercise
    asks you to do with your own story, staged shot by shot.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="rebuild For the Birds, beat by beat" widget="order" config={swBirdsOrder} />
  <p>
    The digital storyboard is where that staging gets designed. Storyboards pre-visualise the
    film as framed drawings (or frames grabbed from a previz tool), numbered by shot; the
    digital workflow adds animatics — the boards cut together with scratch sound and timed —
    which is really a first edit you can watch before shooting a frame. Free tools (such as
    Storyboarder) or even slide-software animatics will do; what matters is that the board
    forces three early decisions: shot size (who owns the frame and when), axis (staging the
    180-degree line before the shoot, not during), and coverage (whether the scene's turns
    are photographable at all). A storyboard is the screenplay's second draft in pictures.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="fill the blanks — the storyboard's three decisions" widget="cloze" config={swBoardCloze} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> that a short film is just a compressed feature. The engines
  differ: features spend their first act building a world, while a short must open with its
  conflict already running and reach a turn inside minutes. Treat a short as a small feature
  and you get the worst of both — a premise too big for its runtime, a runtime too long for
  its premise.</p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="bet before you read on — the compressed feature" widget="predict" config={swShortPredict} />
  <p>
    The logline discipline, worked on the same film: <em>For the Birds</em> compresses to one
    sentence — "smug little birds gang up on an awkward outsider perched on their wire, and
    simple physics makes them pay for it." Every element earns its place: the protagonist
    (the flock, not the big bird — the film's point of view is the majority), the goal (get
    him off the wire), the obstacle (his bulk and their blindness to consequence), and the
    built-in irony (the obstacle is the plot's mechanism). If your short's logline cannot
    carry that load — who, what want, what blocks it, what's the turn of the knife — the
    storyboard will only be drawing the confusion.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> format, structure, scene turns, storyboards and the logline —
    the craft of building a script that survives its first readers. The second half of the
    lesson crosses to the other side of the desk: how the profession evaluates scripts,
    including yours.
  </p>

  <h2>Evaluative analysis: reading scripts like the profession does</h2>
  <p>
    Evaluation is a craft with its own institutions. Development readers produce coverage — a
    synopsis, comments, and a verdict (<em>pass</em>, <em>consider</em>, <em>recommend</em>) —
    and the ten-page reality governs triage: if premise, voice and control have not appeared
    by then, the script rarely gets page eleven. The Black List (Franklin Leonard's annual
    survey of most-liked unproduced scripts, running since 2005) exists precisely because
    evaluation quality decides which scripts live — and it is a reminder that the verdicts are
    opinions with a track record, not measurements.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="call the deadline — the reader's patience" widget="estimate" config={swTenPageEstimate} />
  <p>
    The defensible criteria, for other people's scripts and your own: <strong>(1) the logline
    test</strong> — can the premise be stated in two sentences with protagonist, goal and
    obstacle? If not, the story doesn't know itself yet. <strong>(2) the scene-turn
    test</strong> — does each scene change a value (info, power, safety, intimacy)? Scenes
    that don't turn are deletions waiting to happen. <strong>(3) the want/need audit</strong>
    — is the external goal visibly different from the internal lesson, and does the ending
    know which one it is paying off? <strong>(4) the name-covering dialogue test</strong> —
    cover the character names; if you can't tell who is speaking, the voices aren't built.
    <strong>(5) the theme audit</strong> — what does the ending actually argue, and did the
    climax earn it or simply assert it?
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="pair each criterion with its question" widget="match" config={swCriteriaMatch} />
  <p>
    A worked example that stress-tests the rules: Louis Malle's <em>My Dinner with André</em>
    (1981) is two men talking over dinner for the entire film — a scenario the scene-turn test
    should kill. It works because the turns are positional rather than plot-shaped: Wally
    (the practical New Yorker) resists André's spiritual odyssey beat by beat, and the value
    at stake — how to live — shifts measurably from defence to reflection to (famously) the
    taxi ride home, where Wally rediscovers his coffee cup and his coffee with new eyes. The
    evaluation lesson is double: the criteria are real (the film passes the value-shift test
    in its own currency), and the criteria have limits (a template would have passed on this
    film, and missed it).
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="template turn versus positional turn — drag between them" widget="compare" config={swDinnerCompare} />
  <p>
    The last evaluative skill is receiving and giving notes — the development meeting where
    most scripts are actually improved. The professional distinction is between the
    <em>note</em> and the <em>disease</em>: a reader's "the second act drags" is a symptom
    report, and the fix usually lives somewhere else entirely (a mis-chosen midpoint, a
    protagonist without a want in Act One). Good note-takers describe their experience ("I
    stopped worrying about her here") rather than prescribe rewrites; good note-takers-on
    diagnose before editing, and learn the genre's classic failure modes: the passive
    protagonist (things happen <em>to</em> them until page ninety), the theme stated instead
    of dramatised, the antagonist who is only as smart as the plot requires. Evaluation is a
    conversation, not a score — but every turn of it runs on the same five defensible
    criteria above.
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="the development meeting — trace the note" widget="branch" config={swNotesBranch} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the criteria are real — logline, scene turns, want versus need,
    distinguishable voices, an earned theme — and so are their limits, as <em>My Dinner with
    André</em> proved by passing the value-shift test in its own currency. Two traps remain:
    misreading the verdict, and misreading the template.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> that a pass verdict means the script failed. Coverage
  grades a script <em>for one buyer, today</em> — against their slate, budget, casting
  reality and taste. Scripts pass everywhere and sell nowhere (wrong fit), and are rejected
  by dozens before the right reader finds them; the Black List itself exists because
  "passed on" and "bad" are different judgements. Read verdicts the way you read reviews:
  as one informed opinion, with a context attached.</p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> that structure templates replace judgement. The beats are
  diagnostic instruments — they locate where a draft's energy sags — but they cannot create
  the thing they measure. Field's paradigm tells you where the argument turns; only the
  writer decides whether the turn is true.</p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>State the dramatic engine that sits underneath every structural template, and name the test that checks whether each scene is doing its job.</li>
    <li>Apply the logline test to <em>For the Birds</em>: which elements must the one sentence carry, and what is this film's version of each?</li>
    <li>Why does the lesson treat a coverage verdict as an opinion with a track record rather than a measurement — and why does that make "passed on" different from "bad"?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) A character wants something, something blocks it, and the
    outcome changes a value the audience can feel; the scene-turn test checks each scene for
    a value shift, because scenes that do not turn are deletions waiting to happen. (2) A
    protagonist (here the flock, not the outsider — the film's point of view is the
    majority), a goal (get him off the wire), an obstacle (his bulk and their blindness to
    consequence) and the turn of the knife (the obstacle is the plot's mechanism); if your
    logline cannot carry that load, the storyboard will only draw the confusion. (3) Coverage
    grades a script for one buyer, today — against their slate, budget and taste — so the
    verdict measures fit as much as quality; the Black List itself exists because scripts
    rejected by dozens of readers can still be the best unproduced writing of their year.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>A screenplay is a machine for convincing busy readers; format is the profession's first filter, not a stylistic preference.</li>
    <li>Under every template sits one engine: want, obstacle, change — a value the audience can feel shifting.</li>
    <li>Dialogue is action performed on the listener; a line that exists only to inform the audience is a line the scene does not need.</li>
    <li>Character is choice under cost — the midpoint and "all is lost" beats exist to force decisions that redefine the protagonist.</li>
    <li>A short film is its own discipline: conflict already running, a turn inside minutes, no room for a world-building first act.</li>
    <li>The storyboard is the screenplay's second draft in pictures, and the animatic is a first edit you can watch before shooting.</li>
    <li>Evaluation runs on five defensible criteria — and a verdict is one informed opinion with a context attached.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The other advanced lesson in this tier, Documentary form, shows what happens to the
    want-obstacle-turn engine when the material is found rather than invented — structure
    under the constraint of evidence. And the intermediate Editing the cut lesson pairs
    naturally with this one: the beats you design on the page are, on screen, the cut's
    scheduling decisions.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Three professional frontiers. <strong>The writers' room:</strong> long-form TV authorship
    is collective — staff writers climb to showrunner, and the room's breaking-stories rhythm
    (table, board, cards, outline) is craft at industrial scale, with the Writers Guild's
    minimums as its contract floor. <strong>Adaptation:</strong> the novel's interiority must
    become externalised action — the best adaptations translate <em>theme</em>, not plot (the
    comparative-literature lesson's fidelity question, industrialised), and rights are the
    first page of that process. <strong>The AI question:</strong> the 2023 Writers Guild
    strike — 148 days, May to September — settled the first round: the agreement regulates how
    AI output may be used, protects credit and compensation, and keeps literary material with
    writers; the deeper question (who is an author when a model drafts?) is the same one
    photography asked painting, asked again at keyboard speed. In every branch the engine is
    unchanged: want, obstacle, turn — the template merely counts the beats.
  </p>
  <p>
    One structural extension completes the picture: <strong>series scale</strong>. A television
    pilot does not sell a story; it sells an <em>engine</em> — a configuration of characters,
    pressure and premise that can generate a different conflict every week (or every season)
    without repeating itself. Season architecture stacks the film paradigm one level up:
    episodic turns feed a season-long arc, whose turn lands in the finale. The craft question
    changes accordingly — not "does this story end well?" but "does this machine keep
    producing stories that end well?" — and it is the question the writers' room economy
    above exists to answer.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Snyder, Save the Cat; McKee, Story; Egri, The Art of Dramatic Writing; the literature ladder's plot and character lessons. Source text: OpenStax (https://openstax.org), CC BY 4.0 — adapted for Interactive Atlas.</p>
</TopicPage>
